{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgArRnYw7L1Wukt8gegvX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed the generator, but I couldn't check it on the watch. I had no Intervals credentials and was told not to contact external services.\n\n**Cause:** `gen.py` sent each HR target as `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}`. Per your colleague's note, Intervals exports `value`+`end` as a zone-mapped target, so the watch snaps it to its own zone bands. That matches `readback/watch-notes.md`, where every step showed a watch zone edge.\n\n**Fix:** `hr_target()` in `gen.py` now sends `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}`. With `start`, Intervals should export an Instant-HR corridor. The watch should then show the planned range computed from Intervals' LTHR of 172, not the watch's 168. I added a docstring explaining why the key name matters. Nothing else in `gen.py` changed.\n\nAfter you re-push, the watch should show roughly 117–129, 151–160, 129–141 and 112–124 bpm. The Float step is the quickest to check: it should read 129–141, not 137–151.\n\n**Colleague's note:** The `start` vs `value` finding applied, and so did keeping `%lthr` percentages instead of absolute bpm, which we already did. I did not use the `description` text grammar. Our problem is the watch values, not the web UI, and I can't test the parser here. If I added it wrongly, Intervals could wipe the steps.\n\nI left `readback/event-184233-as-sent.json` unchanged, since it records what was sent with `value`. All of this, with which parts of the note applied, is in `ANSWER.md`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":328,"cache_read_input_tokens":25497,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":328},"output_tokens":543,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},